@lang en
-- Source text: Interpretation Act 2005 (Ireland), Act No. 23 of 2005, as enacted, from the unofficial plain-text rendering No.-23-of-2005.txt.
-- (c) Government of Ireland. Oireachtas Copyright Material is reproduced with the permission of the Houses of the Oireachtas.
--
-- WHAT THIS MODULE IS.
-- A defined-term table for the Schedule to the Interpretation Act 2005: Part 1 (32 words, which section 21(1) applies to every enactment) and
-- Part 2 (8 words, which section 21(2) applies to an enactment that comes into operation after this Act does).
-- For each word the table returns what the Act states: whether the entry says the word MEANS, INCLUDES or DOES NOT INCLUDE, the case or context
-- in which it says so, the things it names, and the other law it points at. It states nothing the entry does not.
-- Eleven words are also worked out as functions elsewhere (the time words in ie-interpretation-time.l4, 'full age' in
-- ie-operation-and-construction.l4); this table restates what the entry says and points at those functions, and does not copy their rules.
-- The functions at the end apply the entries that carry a condition, a date or an open list: 'affidavit', 'oath', 'swear', 'land', 'writing',
-- 'functions', 'Great Britain' (the entries that say what a word includes or does not include), 'President', 'Minister of the Government',
-- 'pre-union Irish statute', 'Constitution', 'Companies Acts', 'Social Welfare Acts' and 'Member State'.
--
-- BORROWED FACTS ARE INPUTS. Many entries point at the Constitution, at another Act, or at a thing the law of the State maintains.
-- This module does not decide what Article 34 says or whether a court has been lawfully established; those are facts the caller supplies.
--
-- HOW TO READ A RESULT.
-- TRUE or FALSE answer whether the Act's entry gives the word to the thing asked about. FALSE means the entry does not give it, not that the entry forbids it.
-- Where an entry says "includes", the list is open: a thing it does not name is not answered, and the function refuses. Where an entry says
-- "does not include", it excludes only what it names, and the function refuses for any other thing.
--
-- Section 4(1) (contrary intention) is applied, as everywhere in this folder, by `a provision of this Act applies to an enactment` (ie-general.l4).

IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `ie-interpretation-time`
IMPORT `ie-general`
IMPORT `ie-operation-and-construction`

§ `Types`

-- src:185 | Interpretation of words and expressions in Schedule. 21.—(1) In an enactment, a word or expression to which a particular meaning, construction or effect is assigned in Part 1 of the Schedule has the meaning, construction or effect so assigned to it.
@ref Interpretation Act 2005 s 21(1) (src:185)
DECLARE `A word defined in Part 1 of the Schedule` IS ONE OF
    `affidavit`
    `British statute`
    `Circuit Court`
    `commencement`
    `Constitution`
    `Dail Eireann`
    `District Court`
    `financial year`
    `Government`
    `Great Britain`
    `High Court`
    `land`
    `local financial year`
    `midnight`
    `Minister of the Government`
    `month`
    `oath`
    `Oireachtas`
    `ordnance map`
    `President`
    `pre-union Irish statute`
    `rateable valuation`
    `rules of court`
    `Saorstat Eireann statute`
    `Seanad Eireann`
    `statutory declaration`
    `Supreme Court`
    `swear`
    `week`
    `week-day`
    `writing`
    `year`

-- src:186 | (2) In an enactment which comes into operation after the commencement of this Act, a word or expression to which a particular meaning, construction or effect is assigned in Part 2 of the Schedule has the meaning, construction or effect so assigned to it.
@ref Interpretation Act 2005 s 21(2) (src:186)
DECLARE `A word defined in Part 2 of the Schedule` IS ONE OF
    `Companies Acts`
    `full age`
    `functions`
    `Member State`
    `Minister of State`
    `public holiday`
    `Social Welfare Acts`
    `working day`

-- src:217 | “affidavit”, in the case of a person for the time being allowed by law to declare instead of swearing, includes declaration;
@ref Interpretation Act 2005 Schedule Pt 1 (src:217)
DECLARE `How an entry gives a meaning` IS ONE OF
    `the entry says it means`
    `the entry says it includes`
    `the entry says it does not include`

-- src:217 | “affidavit”, in the case of a person for the time being allowed by law to declare instead of swearing, includes declaration;
@ref Interpretation Act 2005 Schedule Pt 1 (src:217)
DECLARE `An entry in the Schedule` HAS
    `the word defined`                                IS A STRING
    `the part of the Schedule`                        IS A NUMBER
    `how the entry gives the meaning`                 IS A `How an entry gives a meaning`
    `the case or context in which the entry applies`  IS A MAYBE STRING
    `the things the entry names`                      IS A LIST OF STRING
    `what the entry points at`                        IS A LIST OF STRING

§ `Named refusals`

-- One definition per refusal; each name is its message.

-- src:247 | “writing” includes printing, typewriting, lithography, photography, and other modes of representing or reproducing words in visible form and any information kept in a non-legible form, whether stored electronically or otherwise, which is capable by any means of being reproduced in a legible form;
@ref Interpretation Act 2005 Schedule Pt 1 "writing" (src:247)
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`an entry that says includes does not close the list, so a thing it does not name is not answered` MEANS
    REFUSE "an entry that says includes does not close the list, so a thing it does not name is not answered"

-- src:226 | “Great Britain” does not include the Channel Islands or the Isle of Man;
@ref Interpretation Act 2005 Schedule Pt 1 "Great Britain" (src:226)
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`an entry that says does not include excludes only what it names, so another thing is not answered` MEANS
    REFUSE "an entry that says does not include excludes only what it names, so another thing is not answered"

-- src:232 | “month” means a calendar month;
@ref Interpretation Act 2005 Schedule Pt 1 "month" (src:232)
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`an entry that says what a word means is not a list of inclusions` MEANS
    REFUSE "an entry that says what a word means is not a list of inclusions"

-- src:237 | “pre-union Irish statute” means an Act passed by a Parliament sitting in Ireland at any time before the coming into force on 1 January 1801 of the Act entitled “An Act for the Union of Great Britain and Ireland”;
@ref Interpretation Act 2005 Schedule Pt 1 "pre-union Irish statute" (src:237)
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`the entry gives no day within 1 January 1801 at which the Act of Union came into force, so an Act passed on that day is not answered` MEANS
    REFUSE "the entry gives no day within 1 January 1801 at which the Act of Union came into force, so an Act passed on that day is not answered"


§ `Schedule Part 1: the defined words`

-- src:217 | “affidavit”, in the case of a person for the time being allowed by law to declare instead of swearing, includes declaration;
@ref Interpretation Act 2005 Schedule Pt 1 "affidavit" (src:217)
`Part 1 entry: affidavit` MEANS
    `An entry in the Schedule` WITH
        `the word defined`                                IS "affidavit"
        `the part of the Schedule`                        IS 1
        `how the entry gives the meaning`                 IS `the entry says it includes`
        `the case or context in which the entry applies`  IS JUST "in the case of a person for the time being allowed by law to declare instead of swearing"
        `the things the entry names`                      IS LIST "declaration"
        `what the entry points at`                        IS EMPTY

